NAME
       fstab - the list of file systems mounted at boot

SYNOPSIS
       /etc/fstab

DESCRIPTION
       /etc/fstab tells the system which file systems and swap areas to use
       at every boot, and where. At boot, systemd turns each line into a
       mount (or swap) unit and mounts them in the right order. mount -a
       mounts every listed file system that is not mounted yet, and a mount
       command naming only the mount point (mount /mnt/data) takes the rest
       from this file.

       Each line describes one file system, in six fields separated by spaces
       or tabs. Lines starting with # are comments; empty lines are ignored.

           DEVICE  MOUNT-POINT  TYPE  OPTIONS  DUMP  PASS

   1. DEVICE
       What to mount. Prefer a name that does not change when disks are added
       or reordered:

       UUID=f3c1...   the file system's UUID (blkid shows it). The usual
                      choice on RHEL 9.
       LABEL=data     the file system's label (set with xfs_admin -L,
                      e2label or tune2fs -L, or mkfs -L).
       /dev/VG/LV, /dev/mapper/VG-LV
                      a logical volume; LVM names stay stable.
       /dev/vdb1      a plain device name; may change when disks change.
       HOST:/PATH     an NFS export, such as nfs.lab.example.com:/exports/shared.

   2. MOUNT-POINT
       The directory to mount on. It must exist. For swap write none.

   3. TYPE
       The file system type: xfs, ext4, vfat, nfs (or nfs4), swap, iso9660,
       and others.

   4. OPTIONS
       Comma separated, no spaces. defaults is the usual choice. Common ones:

       defaults      rw, suid, dev, exec, auto, nouser, async.
       ro, rw        read-only, or read-write.
       noauto        do not mount at boot or with mount -a (only by hand).
       nofail        do not stop the boot if the device is missing.
       _netdev       a network file system: wait for the network first
                     (implied for nfs).
       noexec, nosuid, nodev
                     forbid running programs, set-ID bits, device files.
       x-systemd.automount
                     mount only when someone first uses the directory.
       pri=N         for swap: the priority.
       Type-specific options also go here, such as vers=4.2 or sec=sys for
       NFS, or uid=1000,umask=077 for vfat.

   5. DUMP
       Whether the old dump backup program saves it: 0 (always use 0).

   6. PASS
       The order for checking at boot: 0 never check (always for xfs, swap
       and nfs), 1 the root file system, 2 other ext4 file systems.

       After editing fstab, run systemctl daemon-reload so systemd notices,
       and test with mount -a (and swapon -a for swap) before rebooting.

EXAMPLES
       An XFS file system on a partition, by UUID:

           UUID=5b1e9c3a-3d2f-4c55-9d1e-7a0c2b6e1f44  /mnt/data  xfs  defaults  0 0

       An ext4 logical volume:

           /dev/vgdata/lvweb   /var/www   ext4   defaults   0 2

       A file system by label:

           LABEL=archive   /archive   xfs   defaults   0 0

       A swap partition:

           UUID=0e7c8f61-2a4b-4b1e-8f3a-9d2e6c5b7a10  none  swap  defaults  0 0

       An NFS share:

           nfs.lab.example.com:/exports/shared  /mnt/shared  nfs  defaults,_netdev  0 0

       Test the file after editing:

           systemctl daemon-reload
           mount -a
           findmnt --verify

NOTES
       A wrong line (a missing device, a typo in the type) can stop the next
       boot in emergency mode. Always run mount -a after an edit; if it
       complains, fix the line before rebooting. In emergency mode, the root
       password lets you fix the file (mount -o remount,rw / first if needed).

SEE ALSO
       mount(8), umount(8), swapon(8), blkid(8), findmnt(8), nfs(5),
       systemd.mount(5)
